WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.936
There's a huge amount of sturm und drang.

00:00:03.403 --> 00:00:04.270
You know, around

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.872
about what I could do to us

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:07.607
all, to the human race.

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:10.677
I mean, you know, the fears are huge.

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:11.244
To the extent

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:11.945
that the president

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:13.513
is talking to the big bosses,

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:14.981
you're all going to testify

00:00:14.981 --> 00:00:16.182
and you're here

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:17.550
and you're going to Europe, right?

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.218
Yes, that's right.

00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:20.553
You talked about the regulation piece.

00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:22.055
So first, what are you doing here?

00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:23.656
What do you expect to achieve

00:00:23.656 --> 00:00:24.958
here in the UK?

00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:25.925
Well, here in the U.K.

00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:27.927
and I was in Norway on Monday,

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:29.696
the Netherlands on Tuesday, the U.K.

00:00:29.696 --> 00:00:30.830
on Wednesday.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.298
There really is a conversation

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:34.034
taking place around the world

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:35.668
about what to do about A.I.

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:37.470
How do we maximize the benefits?

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:37.971
It will,

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:38.872
I think, help us

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:40.974
diagnosis and cure cancers.

00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:42.609
But then how do we regulate it?

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:44.144
So it's used safely.

00:00:44.144 --> 00:00:45.779
And I think that really does require

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:47.414
that we in the tech sector

00:00:47.414 --> 00:00:48.982
step up, that we put in place

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:50.250
safety protocols

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.252
and safeguards guardrails, if you will.

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:54.054
And it will require,

00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:56.823
I think, a new era of law and regulation.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.725
And that's the conversation

00:00:58.725 --> 00:00:59.926
that somebody like me

00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:01.227
is having around the world.

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:04.230
And now you're continuing that to the EU.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:04.531
Is that

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:06.032
because they are coming up with the

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:08.168
AI Act or the protocols or something?

00:01:08.201 --> 00:01:08.868
What will

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:09.502
I mean,

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:10.770
how much hope do you have for that

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.205
and what will it do?

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:13.973
I am optimistic

00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:17.377
that will strike a sensible balance.

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:18.912
I mean, in the first instance,

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:19.646
I think it's worth

00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:21.815
remembering that we benefit

00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:23.049
and really take for granted

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:25.151
every day technologies

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:27.420
that could have a dangerous impact.

00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:29.589
Electricity turns on the lights,

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.191
but it can electrocute you.

00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:32.792
So we have a circuit breaker.

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:33.993
And so in the same way,

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:36.529
we need to build safety protocols,

00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:38.465
safety brakes, if you will,

00:01:38.465 --> 00:01:39.432
especially if A.I.

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:40.900
is going to be used to control

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.235
critical infrastructure,

00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:44.337
the water supply, the electrical grid.

00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:45.605
And so

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:46.873
what we're focused on

00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:48.041
is inside a company

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:49.309
like Microsoft,

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:52.378
is how we put in air safety guardrails.

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:54.481
The conversation with governments

00:01:54.481 --> 00:01:55.148
is, in effect,

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:56.416
should you as a company

00:01:56.416 --> 00:01:57.450
need to get a license?

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.751
If A.I. is going to be used

00:01:58.751 --> 00:01:59.919
in a certain way?

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:02.288
And this is not, at this point

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:05.158
at least a polarized or partizan debate.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:06.860
Everybody's sort of

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:07.994
rolling up their sleeves

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:09.596
and learning about A.I.

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:11.197
and figuring out how to manage.

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:13.666
So why do you what's your account?

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:14.734
How do you account for that?

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:15.602
That actually

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:17.504
it appears that all different

00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:18.872
parts of the equation

00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:21.107
are thinking along the same lines? Why?

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:23.376
I mean, social media came and, you know,

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:25.178
we all thought it was going to be great,

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:25.411
you know,

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:27.480
the Arab Spring Revolution, Twitter,

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.882
democracy, etc.

00:02:28.882 --> 00:02:29.516
And instead,

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:31.151
it is exponentially worse

00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:32.552
and a considerable

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:34.020
part of our public space.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.222
I think maybe there's a lesson

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:37.423
in what you just said,

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:38.558
because I actually have that

00:02:38.558 --> 00:02:40.026
same conversation.

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:40.627
In some ways,

00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:42.061
we look back a decade ago

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:44.030
and we were all in hindsight,

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.064
too euphoric.

00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:45.965
We thought

00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:46.866
social media

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:48.935
would be the savior of democracy.

00:02:48.935 --> 00:02:51.838
And so you fast forward now, it's 20, 23,

00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:52.906
and we all say,

00:02:52.906 --> 00:02:54.240
let's approach

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.009
this new technology with our eyes

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:56.643
wide open.

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:58.144
It will do amazing things

00:02:58.144 --> 00:02:59.646
that will improve productivity

00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:01.681
and will advance economic growth.

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:02.448
It will be a tool

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:05.084
for teachers and students, but

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:06.386
it has risks.

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:08.188
Let's not put the risks aside

00:03:08.188 --> 00:03:09.822
let's not just say let companies

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:11.057
do whatever they want.

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:13.226
Let's have law that have regulation.

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:16.296
I think that is the right

00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:18.932
kind of wiser approach to take.

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:20.233
Let me again

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:22.402
be the be the devil's advocate.

00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:23.836
Sam Altman, who I know

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:24.671
you're in business with,

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:28.141
you have the shared enterprise open, etc.

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:29.209
He basically said,

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:30.243
and he's been

00:03:30.243 --> 00:03:32.078
front and center in Congress

00:03:32.078 --> 00:03:33.880
and he comes across

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.149
like, you know, the knight

00:03:36.149 --> 00:03:37.350
in shining armor.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.452
But he says about,

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:41.521
you know, the regulation and stuff.

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:42.555
He says, the US Senate.

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:44.691
I think if this technology goes wrong,

00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:45.858
it can go quite wrong.

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:47.160
We want to be vocal about that.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.028
We want to work with the government

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:50.597
to prevent prevent that from happening.

00:03:50.597 --> 00:03:54.267
But then he says that it could cease

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:55.401
his company could cease

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:57.237
operating in the EU.

00:03:57.237 --> 00:03:59.272
If it can't comply with legislation.

00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:01.674
Is he speaking out of ten different sides

00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:02.175
of his mouth,

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:04.043
try to have cake and eat it too?

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:04.811
No, I don't think so.

00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:06.412
I mean, OpenAI, the company

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:07.513
that Sam heads

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:08.881
and Microsoft worked

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:10.383
very closely together.

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:12.318
We've been partners

00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:14.354
and you want a little bit of regulation,

00:04:14.354 --> 00:04:15.355
but not too much.

00:04:15.355 --> 00:04:15.622
Well,

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:16.723
I think, you know, it's

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:19.592
sort of a good thing to think about.

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:21.861
We don't want to stifle innovation.

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:22.629
I think that would

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:24.931
ultimately prevent society

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:26.466
from reaping the benefits

00:04:26.466 --> 00:04:28.501
that this has to offer.

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:30.603
So it's about getting the balance right.

00:04:31.104 --> 00:04:33.072
And it's a time

00:04:33.072 --> 00:04:35.141
when the technology is advancing quickly.

00:04:35.408 --> 00:04:35.942
It's a time

00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:37.610
when people are sitting down together.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:38.911
And not surprisingly,

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:39.746
some days

00:04:39.746 --> 00:04:41.447
it is easier to sort out

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:43.249
an answer than others.

00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:44.083
What you do see,

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:45.451
whether you're talking about

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:47.887
Sam Altman or Microsoft,

00:04:47.887 --> 00:04:48.921
is ultimately

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.256
some convergence,

00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:50.690
you know,

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:53.026
around a set of practical standards.

00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:56.429
And I think in some ways benefiting from

00:04:57.163 --> 00:04:57.964
not just a year,

00:04:57.964 --> 00:04:59.732
but a century's worth of experience.

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:01.734
We all fly on airplanes

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:03.903
if we don't want them to crash.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:05.872
But we want to go farther than we

00:05:05.872 --> 00:05:07.273
otherwise could.

00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:08.241
We have global

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:09.809
as well as national safety

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:11.711
standards, safety features,

00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:13.746
testing before a plane is certified

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:14.647
to fly,

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:16.949
constant monitoring of aircraft

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:19.352
after they've entered service.

00:05:19.352 --> 00:05:20.320
I think that

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.455
points to the right direction.

00:05:22.455 --> 00:05:24.524
We always want better planes.

00:05:24.524 --> 00:05:26.259
We always want them to be safe.

00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:27.927
We should want the same thing for me. I.

